// @observes: LOFT_NO_HALF_LOAD — its output measures the bucket table's byte size, which the load factor that rebuilds it decides.
// @observes: LOFT_NO_POW2_BUCKETS — its output measures the bucket table's byte size, which the growth step decides.
// @observes: LOFT_NO_HASH_INLINE — its output measures the bucket table's byte size, which holds the entries only on an inline table.
// @observes: LOFT_NO_HASH_TAG — its output measures the bucket table's byte size, which a slot's width decides.
//
// @PLN110 sub-arc 1c — `size(hash)` = the full bucket table, HOLES INCLUDED.
// The hash's own allocation is the bucket array: `elms` slots (see @C152 below for what a
// slot holds).  An empty slot is a hole (a zero rec-id) and still counts, because
// open addressing's spare capacity IS the format — unlike a vector, whose spare
// capacity is reserve and is excluded.  Allocation-local: entries that live in the
// collection's entry ARENA are NOT counted; an inline table's are its own (@C152).  The table
// grows in steps as it rehashes (rebuilt at HALF full, room → room·2−1); those step
// sizes encode the resize policy, so a policy change is a conscious layout change
// that flips this test (that is the intent — @PLN97).
//
// @PLN135 arc H moved these numbers once, deliberately: the table reserves FOUR
// words before the bucket array (header, seed, and the arena's directory / cursor /
// free-list / stride) where it used to reserve two, so `elms = (room − 4) · 2`.  The
// first table still starts at 16 slots and the load factor is unchanged — only the
// ladder past it shifts, because a bigger reserved prefix leaves more of each
// doubled record for buckets.
//
// 2026-09-21 moved the POLICY, deliberately (@PLN158, `bench/portal/analysis/keyed.md`
// L5): a table is rebuilt at half full where it was three quarters, because a probe is a
// dependent read of the entry and a miss at 0.75 walks 8.5 buckets.  The ladder is the
// same (16, 38, 82, 170 slots); what moved is WHEN each rung is climbed — the insert that
// finds `length >= elms / 2`.  The first version of this file sampled at 3, 13 and 39
// entries, which both policies answer alike, so the change it exists to notice passed it
// unnoticed; it now samples on both sides of every rung (8|9, 19|20, 41|42), where the
// old rule answers 64, 64, 152, 152, 328, 328.
//
// 2026-10-11 moved the LADDER, deliberately (`@FR-Col-Bucket`, @C146): a table's bucket
// count is a power of two, so the growth step doubles the count — 16, 32, 64, 128 slots —
// where `room → room·2−1` gave 16, 38, 82, 170.  Rebuilt at half full as before, the rungs
// are climbed by the 9th, 17th and 33rd insert; this samples both sides of each (8|9,
// 16|17, 32|33).  `LOFT_NO_POW2_BUCKETS=1` is the old ladder.
//
// @C152 moved the BYTES PER SLOT, deliberately (`(Col-Tag)`, `(Col-Inline)`): a slot is 8 bytes —
// the entry, then its key digest's low half — and a table that owns its entries keeps them
// IN the table, an entry of `stride` bytes per slot.  `K` is two integers, so a slot is
// 8 + 16 = 24 bytes and `size(h)` still counts the table's own allocation (@C150: it reports the
// representation).  The slot counts are what this file pins — 16, 32, 64, 128 — and they did
// not move; `slot` below names the bytes each one costs.
//
// The fills are `while` loops, on purpose: a COUNTED fill (`for i in a..b { h += … }`) may
// reserve its table up front (@C150, `(R-PushFill)`'s hash clause), and `size(h)` then answers
// the reserved table — this file measures the growth ladder an unreserved fill climbs.
//
// Values verified against both backends (interpret + native) during Phase 1.

struct K {
  id: integer,
  v: integer,
}

fn main() {
  slot = 24;  // 8-byte tagged slot + a 16-byte inline `K`
  h: hash<K[id]> = [];
  assert(size(h) == 0, "empty hash = 0 (no table allocated yet): {size(h)}");

  h += K { id: 1, v: 10 };
  h += K { id: 2, v: 20 };
  h += K { id: 3, v: 30 };
  // initial table: 16 slots
  assert(size(h) == 16 * slot, "initial table = 16 slots (13 holes counted): {size(h)}");
  assert(size(h) >= len(h) * slot, "table ≥ live entries × slot (holes make it larger)");

  j = 4;
  while j < 9 {
    h += K { id: j, v: j * 10 };
    j += 1;
  }
  // 8 entries: exactly half of 16 slots, and the table is still the first one — the
  // rebuild belongs to the insert that FINDS it half full.
  assert(size(h) == 16 * slot, "8 entries still fit the first table: {size(h)}");
  h += K { id: 9, v: 90 };
  // first rehash: 16 → 32 slots
  assert(size(h) == 32 * slot, "the 9th insert rebuilds: 32 slots: {size(h)}");

  j = 10;
  while j < 17 {
    h += K { id: j, v: j * 10 };
    j += 1;
  }
  assert(size(h) == 32 * slot, "16 entries = half of 32: not yet: {size(h)}");
  h += K { id: 17, v: 170 };
  // second rehash: 32 → 64 slots
  assert(size(h) == 64 * slot, "the 17th insert rebuilds: 64 slots: {size(h)}");

  j = 18;
  while j < 32 {
    h += K { id: j, v: j * 10 };
    j += 1;
  }
  // the full table exceeds the live count: 31 live entries in a 64-slot table
  assert(
    len(h) == 31 && size(h) / slot == 64,
    "64-slot table holds 31 live: len={len(h)} slots={size(h) / slot}"
  );
  h += K { id: 32, v: 320 };
  assert(size(h) == 64 * slot, "32 entries = half of 64: not yet: {size(h)}");
  h += K { id: 33, v: 330 };
  // third rehash: 64 → 128 slots
  assert(size(h) == 128 * slot, "the 33rd insert rebuilds: 128 slots: {size(h)}");
  // every key is still found after three rebuilds
  found = 0;
  for i in 1..34 { if h[i] != null { found += 1; } }
  assert(found == 33, "all 33 keys answer after three rebuilds: {found}");
}
